{"items": [{"shortCode": "De4MKCpRKiX", "url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/De4MKCpRKiX/", "caption": "Microsoft has been working on Project Silica for nearly a decade, and this month marked its biggest leap yet.\n\nThe research phase is now officially complete, and the system no longer requires rare, expensive glass. \n\nIt now works with the same heat-resistant material found in Pyrex cookware. That solves what was previously the biggest barrier to real-world deployment. \n\nThe glass stores nearly 5 terabytes of data in a coaster-sized slab, needs no power to maintain it, and is immune to heat, water, and magnetic fields that would destroy conventional storage within decades.\n\nMicrosoft has already used it to archive the 1978 Superman film and partnered with the Global Music Vault to preserve recordings under Arctic ice.\n\nYou can read more about it in their latest research paper, details below\ud83d\udc47\n\nStudy: \u201cLaser writing in glass for dense, fast and efficient archival data storage\u201d published in Nature\nDOI: 10.1038/s41586-025-10042-w", "timestamp": "2026-10-24T13:00:21.000Z", "videoDuration": 74.666664, "videoPlayCount": 1748, "videoViewCount": null, "likesCount": 38, "commentsCount": 1, "displayUrl": "https://scontent-fra3-2.cdninstagram.com/v/t51.71878-15/861637494_974022152450526_6591416224601214522_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e15_tt6&_nc_cat=107&ig_cache_key=NDAxNTAxMjUzNDM5MzU0NjkwMzI4OTE1NzU0OTk4MDY2MDg4.3-ccb7-5&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=58cdad&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6IkNMSVBTLnhwaWRzLjY0MC5zZHIudmlkZW9fZGVmYXVsdF9jb3Zlcl9mcmFtZS5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=C48iqv9YA0QQ7kNvwHCtqkJ&_nc_oc=Adp01RJ7VKXLl3pfS5CITojs91EKZAiVecChOSB5O9ywp8OxnXq1FK0GDN6B2fi8vpIzZJMV3TzedJMXKy7maxnj&_nc_ad=z-m&_nc_cid=0&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-fra3-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=-BGjuqQcKiwamHTOvtPk0w&_nc_ss=7a20f&oh=00_AQPuoDdDlONoesE1cMDLvDqyWNNUHWklEemzK5-OOQv7cQ&oe=6AE51967", "videoUrl": "https://scontent-fra5-1.cdninstagram.com/o1/v/t2/f2/m86/AQOkI244ul53oUgVi1OURlvQe5f1VFBXEw5R0pDPrZWmZiC3vuRjGNGyHqX0bTRKGCaW0k39zoMNbIbvTX6aXBqbmvci8uJ1RN2bONs.mp4?_nc_cat=110&_nc_sid=5e9851&_nc_ht=scontent-fra5-1.cdninstagram.com&_nc_ohc=HPiRjmW6_bMQ7kNvwGvlqNp&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5JTlNUQUdSQU0uQ0xJUFMuQzMuNzIwLmRhc2hfYmFzZWxpbmVfMV92MSIsInhwdl9hc3NldF9pZCI6Mjg5MTU3MzM1MTgwNjgyMzYsImFzc2V0X2FnZV9kYXlzIjoyLCJ2aV91c2VjYXNlX2lkIjoxMDA5OSwiZHVyYXRpb25fcyI6NzQsInVybGdlbl9zb3VyY2UiOiJ3d3cifQ%3D%3D&ccb=17-1&vs=b60b98d0ec9f852a&_nc_&_nc_gid=-BGjuqQcKiwamHTOvtPk0w&_nc_ss=70580&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQMHTc1w1Kk8JbsCaWQ8FCh7kYbwWJ6tWhgaY0W6xQHjAQ&oe=6AE13327", "transcript": null, "ownerUsername": "rowancheung", "ownerFullName": "Rowan Cheung"}, {"shortCode": "De2UIwTu1ZX", "url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/De2UIwTu1ZX/", "caption": "\ud83e\udd82 Claude just helped design a potential scorpion antivenom. \n\nIn 64 hours, an internal research model generated over 318,000 protein designs, guided by five human messages. \n\nAnthropic\u2019s scientists and Citrini Research sent 211 designs to outside labs for testing and 17 met the study\u2019s binding criteria. \n\nThese tiny proteins grab scorpion toxins, potentially blocking their attack on nerve cells. \n\nBut binding isn\u2019t the same as neutralizing venom\u2026\n\nThe team hasn\u2019t tested whether they protect cells or animals, and each successful design bound only one toxin. \n\nIt\u2019s an early research result with a much bigger question ahead. Can these molecules eventually become safe and effective medicines?\n\nResearch paper: \u201cDe novo design of protein binders against scorpion toxins with Claude\u201d", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T19:33:17.000Z", "videoDuration": 69.76667, "videoPlayCount": 50846, "videoViewCount": null, "likesCount": 957, "commentsCount": 12, "displayUrl": "https://instagram.frtm1-2.fna.fbcdn.net/v/t51.82787-15/865215623_18022456829944561_853607599285366751_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e15_tt6&_nc_cat=100&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ig_cache_key=NDAxNDQ4NDY4MDQwNDA2MzgzMTE4MDIyNDU2ODI2